1
00:00:00,840 --> 00:00:03,460
The violence and slaughter of war.

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:06,700
The urgency and hope of medicine.

3
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:10,820
From the field surgeries of the ancient world,

4
00:00:11,400 --> 00:00:14,020
to the amputation tables of the Civil War,

5
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:17,180
to the medevac dramas of Vietnam,

6
00:00:18,280 --> 00:00:22,620
an army within all armies has fought a war within all wars,

7
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:26,340
trying to revive the wounded and save lives.

8
00:00:26,341 --> 00:00:30,720
Now, Battlefield Medicine on Modern Marvels.

9
00:00:42,060 --> 00:00:47,180
He who would become a surgeon should join the army and follow it.

10
00:00:47,700 --> 00:00:52,540
So Hippocrates, father of medicine, counseled nearly 2,500 years ago.

11
00:00:52,541 --> 00:00:56,780
If war has a silver lining, perhaps this is it.

12
00:00:56,781 --> 00:01:01,740
It's the ultimate hands-on classroom in raw anatomy and mutilated flesh.

13
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:09,660
A horrific classroom that teaches above all what the human species has been willing to do to itself.

14
00:01:10,660 --> 00:01:15,780
There is something paradoxical about the very idea of battlefield medicine,

15
00:01:15,781 --> 00:01:23,040
about armies and individuals desperately trying to kill and rescue at the same time.

16
00:01:24,440 --> 00:01:29,520
Louis Pasteur, the famous scientist, said that there are two forces in this world

17
00:01:29,521 --> 00:01:31,420
that are constantly at play.

18
00:01:31,940 --> 00:01:36,460
One is a force of always designing new weapons of destruction

19
00:01:36,461 --> 00:01:38,360
and some of them of mass destruction.

20
00:01:38,361 --> 00:01:43,180
And the other force is always trying to find new cures for diseases

21
00:01:43,181 --> 00:01:45,280
and how to save lives.

22
00:01:45,780 --> 00:01:50,020
And he said, which one of these forces will win out at the end, God alone knows.

23
00:01:50,900 --> 00:01:54,860
It may seem like mass destruction has gotten the upper hand.

24
00:01:56,200 --> 00:02:00,520
Modern weapons make the very notion of battlefield medicine seem obsolete.

25
00:02:01,900 --> 00:02:05,360
But the weapons and strategies of rescue have also evolved,

26
00:02:05,920 --> 00:02:09,760
benefiting at times from the same technology that propels the slaughter.

27
00:02:09,761 --> 00:02:17,260
The history of battlefield medicine is almost as old as the history of organized war.

28
00:02:18,260 --> 00:02:23,720
1,000 years before the birth of Christ, the legendary Greek hero Achilles took time to

29
00:02:23,721 --> 00:02:28,220
bandage the wounds of his comrade Patroclus during the Trojan War.

30
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:32,880
According to Homer, ancient soldiers rarely survived their wounds.

31
00:02:34,400 --> 00:02:39,000
192 of the 213 casualties he described in the Iliad died.

32
00:02:39,760 --> 00:02:43,280
Things improved dramatically in the Roman Empire,

33
00:02:43,600 --> 00:02:46,280
where soldiers enjoyed such good battlefield care

34
00:02:46,281 --> 00:02:49,060
that they lived five years longer than civilians.

35
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:54,360
The Roman military medical system was without question

36
00:02:54,361 --> 00:02:56,820
the most developed in the ancient world.

37
00:02:57,380 --> 00:03:00,560
In fact, it would not see its equal for over a millennium.

38
00:03:01,460 --> 00:03:05,460
It's very clear from evidence like Trajan's column

39
00:03:05,461 --> 00:03:10,840
that there are soldiers who are assigned to care for their colleagues

40
00:03:10,841 --> 00:03:12,760
during and or after the battle.

41
00:03:12,761 --> 00:03:17,340
There's also evidence that there were physicians attached to legions

42
00:03:17,341 --> 00:03:21,260
suggesting a hierarchical system of care

43
00:03:21,261 --> 00:03:24,520
back through the chain of evacuation.

44
00:03:25,160 --> 00:03:28,120
All that disappeared during the Dark Ages,

45
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:30,880
when armies organized nothing for the wounded.

46
00:03:31,620 --> 00:03:34,840
During the Crusades, knights either brought their own physicians

47
00:03:34,841 --> 00:03:38,980
to battle or were left to die where they fell.

48
00:03:38,981 --> 00:03:43,120
Gunpowder, first used in 1325, created ghastly

49
00:03:43,121 --> 00:03:46,892
new wounds and a new urgency for military surgery.

50
00:03:46,893 --> 00:03:50,060
But ignorance led to appalling procedures.

51
00:03:50,720 --> 00:03:54,120
Gunpowder wounds were considered poisonous and treated accordingly.

52
00:03:54,840 --> 00:03:57,155
You have to get rid of the poison.

53
00:03:57,156 --> 00:04:00,560
Heat is usually the best way to overcome a poison.

54
00:04:00,561 --> 00:04:07,380
and so cautery, boiling oil, other fairly heroic approaches

55
00:04:07,381 --> 00:04:10,840
were taken to treating these wounds.

56
00:04:11,260 --> 00:04:15,940
Boiling oil or cautery with a hot iron tended to destroy tissue

57
00:04:15,941 --> 00:04:22,060
and by the 14th century amputees were a familiar part of the European landscape.

58
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:30,500
This wound man, riddled with knife, sword, axe, arrow, truncheon and gunball

59
00:04:30,501 --> 00:04:36,500
wounds to major arteries, was used to train 16th century battlefield surgeons.

60
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:43,740
The most celebrated was a Frenchman named Ambrose Paré. It was Paré who sometimes

61
00:04:43,741 --> 00:04:49,360
performed surgeries in the midst of battle, who revolutionized the treatment of gunshot wounds

62
00:04:49,361 --> 00:04:52,859
using herbal salves instead of boiling oil.

63
00:04:52,860 --> 00:04:56,520
In addition, he devised pioneering techniques

64
00:04:56,521 --> 00:04:58,620
in amputation and hemorrhage control,

65
00:04:59,180 --> 00:05:01,160
and even ventured into plastic surgery,

66
00:05:01,720 --> 00:05:04,660
designing replacement noses complete with mustache.

67
00:05:06,060 --> 00:05:09,160
Modern combat medicine begins with the French Revolution

68
00:05:09,161 --> 00:05:11,980
and a doctor named Dominique Jean Larre.

69
00:05:12,660 --> 00:05:15,980
The big thing that Larre did, beyond organization,

70
00:05:16,800 --> 00:05:18,020
beyond being a great surgeon,

71
00:05:18,440 --> 00:05:21,120
was he brought the surgeon to the soldier.

72
00:05:21,700 --> 00:05:23,520
He didn't have the surgeon set

73
00:05:23,521 --> 00:05:25,940
and wait till somebody dragged a soldier to him.

74
00:05:26,520 --> 00:05:32,480
To that end, Larre devised the Ambulance Volante, or Flying Ambulance,

75
00:05:32,720 --> 00:05:35,520
which could whisk casualties out of harm's way.

76
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:40,180
He also created teams of ambulance drivers and litter bears

77
00:05:40,181 --> 00:05:44,280
whose sole duty was to find wounded men and bring them to safety.

78
00:05:44,920 --> 00:05:50,360
The treatment occurs much sooner, hours, sometimes days sooner,

79
00:05:51,080 --> 00:05:55,840
in a profession where hours make a difference between life and death.

80
00:05:56,520 --> 00:06:01,920
The Industrial Revolution of the 19th century created new possibilities for mass destruction.

81
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:08,120
Statistically, the American Civil War was the most life-threatening war of all time.

82
00:06:08,760 --> 00:06:13,400
A quarter of those wounded in action died, 620,000 in all.

83
00:06:13,900 --> 00:06:16,620
130,000 men were disfigured for life.

84
00:06:17,000 --> 00:06:19,080
50,000 were amputees.

85
00:06:19,081 --> 00:06:25,240
A new bullet, the mini-ball, conically shaped and three times larger than the musket ball,

86
00:06:25,241 --> 00:06:27,880
was responsible for much of the carnage.

87
00:06:28,880 --> 00:06:31,760
When the miniball hit flesh,

88
00:06:32,240 --> 00:06:36,180
it caused a rapidly expanding wound

89
00:06:36,181 --> 00:06:39,400
so that you could put your fist in the hole

90
00:06:39,401 --> 00:06:40,860
where it came out sometime.

91
00:06:41,620 --> 00:06:44,860
It would shatter bones and leave wounds

92
00:06:44,861 --> 00:06:48,580
unlike anything surgeons had seen in their civilian practices.

93
00:06:49,320 --> 00:06:52,940
Because of the severity of the wounds and the danger of infection,

94
00:06:53,700 --> 00:06:57,260
A shot to the limbs meant almost certain amputation.

95
00:06:58,160 --> 00:07:06,000
90% of the legs or arms that were injured with ballistics had to be amputated.

96
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:10,640
Only if it was just a flesh wound, up to 20 to 30% of people died.

97
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:16,720
As the war progressed, field surgeons became adept at hacking off limbs in under a minute.

98
00:07:17,600 --> 00:07:21,480
A morning's work is the ironic title of this union photo.

99
00:07:22,940 --> 00:07:25,840
Just years away from the germ theory of disease,

100
00:07:26,260 --> 00:07:29,660
amputations were performed with little concern for cleanliness.

101
00:07:30,680 --> 00:07:35,600
There wasn't much time, and so a surgeon would change an apron

102
00:07:35,601 --> 00:07:38,260
when it was so bloody as to mess up his own clothes.

103
00:07:38,660 --> 00:07:42,940
If his knife got dull, he would strop it on the nearest available leather,

104
00:07:43,460 --> 00:07:46,960
perhaps his boot, because there was no reason not to,

105
00:07:47,220 --> 00:07:50,440
and it saved time, and time was obviously crucial.

106
00:07:51,420 --> 00:07:54,780
Recuperation in a permanent hospital was equally hazardous.

107
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:01,900
It was seven times safer to fight through all three days of Gettysburg, an army sergeant wrote,

108
00:08:02,400 --> 00:08:05,420
than to have an arm or leg cut off and be treated in a hospital.

109
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:11,940
Recently discovered anesthetics like ether and chloroform were in short supply,

110
00:08:11,941 --> 00:08:16,640
and fully half the amputations were done to conscious soldiers.

111
00:08:16,641 --> 00:08:23,220
Quack medicines abounded, like Dolly's magical pain extractor

112
00:08:23,221 --> 00:08:28,180
Or Holloway's pills, promising health for the soldier

113
00:08:28,181 --> 00:08:35,100
Disease, as in all wars to that time, claimed far more lives than guns

114
00:08:35,101 --> 00:08:39,780
Malaria, typhoid fever and dysentery swept through the Union and rebel armies

115
00:08:39,781 --> 00:08:46,340
Composed largely of farm boys who caught every bug that camp life and battlefield could offer

116
00:08:47,300 --> 00:08:53,720
Worst of all, as the war began, neither side was remotely prepared for dealing with massive casualties.

117
00:08:54,560 --> 00:09:01,020
The North had less than 100 doctors on staff and no strategy for evacuation or casualty care.

118
00:09:01,320 --> 00:09:06,740
The first big battle, Manassas-Bull Run, in July of 1861,

119
00:09:07,340 --> 00:09:12,200
patients were left on the battlefield on Union side up to a week before they were picked up.

120
00:09:12,201 --> 00:09:15,980
There were no general hospitals. There were no field hospitals.

121
00:09:16,140 --> 00:09:18,280
Regimental surgeons had not been instructed.

122
00:09:18,860 --> 00:09:22,260
It was, in short, no system for taking care of the wounded.

123
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:29,240
A system came, unexpectedly, courtesy of a 37-year-old doctor named Jonathan Letterman,

124
00:09:29,540 --> 00:09:34,580
who was put in charge of medical care for the Army of the Potomac in 1862.

125
00:09:35,520 --> 00:09:41,080
Building on the innovations of the Frenchman Larré, Letterman improved upon the ambulance corps,

126
00:09:41,081 --> 00:09:47,540
replaced civilian drivers with soldiers, and created an all-important chain of evacuation.

127
00:09:48,800 --> 00:09:54,240
Casualties were rushed from the battlefield to nearby dressing stations to be patched up and stabilized.

128
00:09:55,160 --> 00:10:01,800
Then, if necessary, moved to field hospitals, and finally to fixed hospitals in big cities for recuperation.

129
00:10:03,360 --> 00:10:07,200
Trains played an important role in evacuation, as did riverboats,

130
00:10:07,201 --> 00:10:11,220
which were transformed into state-of-the-art floating hospitals

131
00:10:11,221 --> 00:10:14,400
that evacuated 150,000 men.

132
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:20,980
Most importantly, Letterman put medical officers in control of supplies

133
00:10:20,981 --> 00:10:25,600
so that they no longer had to beg for wagons, bandages, and men.

134
00:10:26,600 --> 00:10:32,960
He put all of this together in a way to run a medical department

135
00:10:32,961 --> 00:10:38,060
under medical control to support the commander, the soldier, and the war.

136
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:42,020
Everything that Letterman looked at, all of his changes,

137
00:10:42,240 --> 00:10:47,320
all of his innovations are routine now, and they were not routine then.

138
00:10:48,680 --> 00:10:54,760
Letterman's revolutionary system was first put to the test in Antietam in 1862.

139
00:10:54,761 --> 00:10:57,960
On a battlefield that stretched six miles,

140
00:10:58,600 --> 00:11:01,920
every wounded Union man was treated within 36 hours

141
00:11:01,921 --> 00:11:05,220
By war's end, casualties were reached in half a day.

142
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:11,980
Bloody as it was, the Civil War marked the birth of modern, organized combat medicine.

143
00:11:12,680 --> 00:11:17,360
The transition would prove vital as war entered a new global stage.

144
00:11:22,000 --> 00:11:27,180
World War I dwarfed the Civil War in scale of combat and casualties.

145
00:11:27,181 --> 00:11:34,180
new weapons created a degree of slaughter inconceivable in previous epics.

146
00:11:34,181 --> 00:11:40,180
But the devastation would have been much worse had not the war brought modern medicine to the battlefield,

147
00:11:40,181 --> 00:11:47,180
germ-free surgeries, pioneering new drugs, x-ray technology and blood transfusion.

148
00:11:47,181 --> 00:11:52,180
In addition, the First World War raised popular awareness of combat medicine to a new high

149
00:11:52,181 --> 00:11:55,440
and mark the arrival of a new kind of soldier,

150
00:11:56,100 --> 00:11:58,600
a soldier who did not carry a weapon.

151
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:04,300
World War I gives us our modern image

152
00:12:04,301 --> 00:12:07,460
of the enlisted medical soldier,

153
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:11,380
corpsman, medic, call him what you will.

154
00:12:12,020 --> 00:12:14,680
For the first time in a significant war,

155
00:12:15,380 --> 00:12:18,140
enlisted medical personnel are assigned

156
00:12:18,141 --> 00:12:21,440
not in the safety of the hospital and the ambulance train,

157
00:12:21,441 --> 00:12:25,920
but with the infantry far forward to take care of the wounded.

158
00:12:26,640 --> 00:12:29,200
Their job treating men where they fell,

159
00:12:29,820 --> 00:12:33,880
in the muddy wasteland between the trenches known as no man's land,

160
00:12:34,380 --> 00:12:35,600
was full of hazard.

161
00:12:36,380 --> 00:12:42,720
The mud, the gore, machine gun bullets and gas, all there.

162
00:12:43,320 --> 00:12:47,820
Your task is to go out ankle deep sometimes in mud,

163
00:12:47,821 --> 00:12:55,480
pick up the wounded, drag them, carry them back to the safety of the trench.

164
00:12:56,320 --> 00:13:02,420
It's an almost impossible job, and medics die in disproportionate numbers.

165
00:13:03,440 --> 00:13:07,740
Occasionally, the trenches were rigged with trolleys to transport the wounded.

166
00:13:08,480 --> 00:13:14,300
Most of the time, however, litter bearers hauled them through mud, snow, mountains,

167
00:13:14,301 --> 00:13:17,140
and other torturous terrain.

168
00:13:17,820 --> 00:13:26,820
Casualties were brought to first aid bunkers, often burrowed out of cliffs and packed with sandbags, or improvised in churches.

169
00:13:29,820 --> 00:13:34,820
A fleet of ambulances rushed the severely wounded to hospitals.

170
00:13:35,820 --> 00:13:41,820
Cars were useless on much of the terrain, and 83 different kinds of ambulance were employed,

171
00:13:42,820 --> 00:13:45,820
everything from horse-drawn wagon to tank-like armored car.

172
00:13:45,821 --> 00:13:53,740
car trench life itself produced many casualties think about living in a hole in the ground

173
00:13:54,460 --> 00:14:04,380
month after month it rains it snows you're cold you're wet living in the trench is the worst

174
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:11,900
of your nightmare of camping magnified by days and weeks of doing it with other people

175
00:14:11,901 --> 00:14:18,680
The unending cold and wet produced a debilitating condition known as trench foot.

176
00:14:19,460 --> 00:14:27,400
And the heavily manured trench soil gave off a foul gas that infected wounds and led to deadly gangrene.

177
00:14:29,260 --> 00:14:34,540
Adding to the difficulties a new weapon and a new ordeal for medical personnel.

178
00:14:35,560 --> 00:14:37,780
Man-made poison gas.

179
00:14:37,781 --> 00:14:47,180
The Germans were the first to use gas, releasing a cloud of chlorine outside the French village of Ypres on April 22, 1915.

180
00:14:47,980 --> 00:14:51,560
The fumes killed nearly a thousand men within minutes.

181
00:14:52,640 --> 00:14:57,560
It was a terror weapon. People saw it. They saw their friends falling down from it.

182
00:14:58,260 --> 00:14:59,600
They simply panicked and ran.

183
00:14:59,601 --> 00:15:04,160
people who stayed and tried to fight on

184
00:15:04,161 --> 00:15:06,040
obviously got the casualties

185
00:15:06,041 --> 00:15:08,700
the burning of the eyes

186
00:15:08,701 --> 00:15:10,500
the burning of the mouth

187
00:15:10,501 --> 00:15:11,760
in the chest

188
00:15:11,761 --> 00:15:14,160
and eventually the

189
00:15:14,161 --> 00:15:16,480
accumulation of fluid in the lungs

190
00:15:16,481 --> 00:15:18,060
that led to death

191
00:15:18,061 --> 00:15:20,840
gas masks worn by animals

192
00:15:20,841 --> 00:15:21,780
as well as men

193
00:15:21,781 --> 00:15:24,240
were the primary defense against this

194
00:15:24,241 --> 00:15:25,920
insidious new threat

195
00:15:25,921 --> 00:15:28,540
gassed soldiers

196
00:15:28,541 --> 00:15:31,060
had to be scrubbed down as quickly as possible,

197
00:15:31,660 --> 00:15:34,680
and special ambulances were rigged with shower units

198
00:15:34,681 --> 00:15:35,980
to speed the process.

199
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:39,560
But the new weapon was sickeningly effective,

200
00:15:40,320 --> 00:15:42,980
blinding thousands and accounting for one-third

201
00:15:42,981 --> 00:15:45,180
of all casualties during the war.

202
00:15:46,620 --> 00:15:49,780
Mustard gas, which remained active in clothes and equipment

203
00:15:49,781 --> 00:15:52,300
for months, was especially debilitating.

204
00:15:52,301 --> 00:15:55,340
The First World War, with its unique horrors,

205
00:15:55,341 --> 00:15:58,620
also marked the appearance, in alarming numbers,

206
00:15:59,140 --> 00:16:03,380
of a new kind of casualty, a psychological casualty.

207
00:16:03,381 --> 00:16:10,100
You're just worn out by the tension of hour-by-hour fear,

208
00:16:11,100 --> 00:16:15,580
terror, danger, seeing your buddies killed.

209
00:16:16,220 --> 00:16:19,500
Patients began to break psychically.

210
00:16:20,100 --> 00:16:21,540
They simply couldn't function.

211
00:16:22,040 --> 00:16:23,400
They would break down and cry.

212
00:16:23,740 --> 00:16:24,500
They would scream.

213
00:16:24,501 --> 00:16:29,560
They would stare off in what became called the thousand-yard stare.

214
00:16:30,160 --> 00:16:35,060
Eventually, shell shock, as it was called, was recognized as a war disease,

215
00:16:35,480 --> 00:16:40,760
and soldiers with wounded psyches were evacuated along with the physically wounded men.

216
00:16:41,920 --> 00:16:44,820
Despite the gruesome scale of mutilation and death,

217
00:16:45,280 --> 00:16:49,800
World War I was a turning point in the history of battlefield medicine.

218
00:16:49,801 --> 00:16:55,460
On average, wounded men were brought to field hospitals within five hours,

219
00:16:55,980 --> 00:16:59,240
and if necessary, to better equip fixed hospitals in ten.

220
00:17:00,420 --> 00:17:05,400
X-ray machines, sometimes portable, were used on a mass scale for the first time,

221
00:17:05,900 --> 00:17:08,800
helping surgeons avoid unnecessary operations.

222
00:17:09,680 --> 00:17:14,000
The germ theory and antisepsis paved the way for modern hygiene,

223
00:17:14,500 --> 00:17:18,240
and patients no longer had to fear hospitals and infection.

224
00:17:18,980 --> 00:17:22,140
Mobile laboratories tested bacteria along the front,

225
00:17:22,141 --> 00:17:28,400
and a pioneering generation of inoculations helped keep a host of historic plagues under control.

226
00:17:29,400 --> 00:17:36,080
World War I was the first war in history in which microbes did not kill more men than weapons.

227
00:17:39,300 --> 00:17:44,640
One positive outgrowth of the butchery of this war was plastic surgery.

228
00:17:45,560 --> 00:17:50,460
Exploding artillery produced an unprecedented number of gruesome facial injuries.

229
00:17:51,460 --> 00:17:58,540
At first, wounded men were fitted with lifelike masks to cover up blown-off noses, lips, and eyes.

230
00:17:59,920 --> 00:18:04,780
But the epidemic of disfigurement provided a laboratory for surgeons

231
00:18:04,781 --> 00:18:08,400
who developed groundbreaking procedures for facial reconstruction,

232
00:18:09,060 --> 00:18:12,840
and by war's end, a new field of medicine had been forged.

233
00:18:14,640 --> 00:18:20,280
An American surgeon by the name of Oswald Robertson was responsible for a more critical breakthrough.

234
00:18:20,960 --> 00:18:26,380
While treating men on the front in France, he improvised the world's first blood bank.

235
00:18:26,820 --> 00:18:29,720
He collected universal donor blood into glass bottles.

236
00:18:29,721 --> 00:18:36,720
He stored these in a wooden box that he built out of two ammunition cases and filled the space in between with sawdust.

237
00:18:37,920 --> 00:18:41,320
Put a biscuit tin in the middle and punched holes in it and filled it full of ice.

238
00:18:41,321 --> 00:18:46,520
and dragged 22 bottles of blood that he'd collected

239
00:18:46,521 --> 00:18:50,260
from recovering casualties and people around the hospital

240
00:18:50,261 --> 00:18:53,280
forward to a British clearing station.

241
00:18:54,120 --> 00:18:56,980
Robertson gave the blood to a group of 20 soldiers

242
00:18:56,981 --> 00:19:01,460
who had been triaged as expectant, that is, expected to die.

243
00:19:02,280 --> 00:19:05,500
Nine of them lived, and the new weapon of blood banking

244
00:19:05,501 --> 00:19:07,800
and combat transfusion was born.

245
00:19:07,801 --> 00:19:14,760
It was a weapon that would be put to the test on a massive scale in the next world war.

246
00:19:16,360 --> 00:19:28,400
The influenza epidemic of 1918, which killed over 20 million people around the world, swept through the trenches of France and immobilized armies on every side.

247
00:19:29,200 --> 00:19:33,260
Battlefield Medicine will continue in a moment on Modern Marvels.

248
00:19:33,261 --> 00:19:43,460
A single generation after the First World War ended, a Second World War began.

249
00:19:44,460 --> 00:19:48,760
Once again, there was a quantum leap in the technology of death.

250
00:19:50,520 --> 00:19:55,540
But combat medicine also benefited from new weapons and tactics.

251
00:19:58,760 --> 00:20:02,820
From Guadalcanal to the beaches of Normandy.

252
00:20:03,260 --> 00:20:07,500
in surgical tents and improvised hospitals.

253
00:20:08,180 --> 00:20:12,580
Medics and doctors reached wounded men faster and more effectively than ever before.

254
00:20:14,440 --> 00:20:18,860
One of the symbols of military medicine in World War II was plasma.

255
00:20:20,120 --> 00:20:23,580
Whole blood transfusion pioneered during the First World War

256
00:20:23,581 --> 00:20:26,600
was still considered impractical on a mass scale

257
00:20:26,601 --> 00:20:30,040
because blood spoiled easily and was difficult to store.

258
00:20:30,640 --> 00:20:33,980
But plasma, liquid blood minus the red blood cells,

259
00:20:34,080 --> 00:20:37,300
could be freeze-dried and canned, carried easily,

260
00:20:37,820 --> 00:20:40,240
and used to keep hemorrhaging men alive.

261
00:20:41,060 --> 00:20:45,880
The use of plasma for initial treatment of shock saved any number of lives.

262
00:20:46,280 --> 00:20:47,800
Corman could give it right up front.

263
00:20:48,600 --> 00:20:50,220
Didn't have to be refrigerated.

264
00:20:50,320 --> 00:20:51,880
You had a tin can, you took it apart,

265
00:20:52,320 --> 00:20:55,220
you put the water into the lyophilized plasma,

266
00:20:55,221 --> 00:20:57,480
you swirled it around, you stuck it in the IV thing,

267
00:20:57,540 --> 00:20:58,780
and you gave it to the guy.

268
00:20:58,781 --> 00:21:04,060
Unfortunately, plasma had some serious, if little publicized, defects.

269
00:21:04,960 --> 00:21:07,380
Unlike whole blood, plasma was pooled,

270
00:21:07,880 --> 00:21:12,140
and before long the entire supply was contaminated with hepatitis.

271
00:21:13,040 --> 00:21:17,060
In mid-war, after tens of thousands of soldiers came down with the disease,

272
00:21:17,520 --> 00:21:20,440
the Army decided to switch from plasma to blood.

273
00:21:21,540 --> 00:21:24,580
Charles Drew, a leading expert on blood banking,

274
00:21:24,581 --> 00:21:30,260
was given the crucial task of developing a nationwide blood banking system overnight.

275
00:21:31,620 --> 00:21:36,900
What Drew did was an enormous accomplishment because it had never been done before

276
00:21:36,901 --> 00:21:43,600
where strangers came in to give blood to strangers in advance of anyone needing the blood.

277
00:21:44,480 --> 00:21:50,220
But now you have thousands of people for patriotic reasons coming in to give blood

278
00:21:50,221 --> 00:21:55,000
for soldiers, sailors, airmen, and marines that they'll never see.

279
00:21:55,680 --> 00:21:59,020
Ironically, the blood that Charles Drew, a black man, collected

280
00:21:59,021 --> 00:22:01,820
was racially segregated by the military.

281
00:22:02,400 --> 00:22:05,060
No white person should have Negro blood.

282
00:22:05,800 --> 00:22:08,120
That was a given, not arguable.

283
00:22:08,860 --> 00:22:10,360
Red Cross fought it tooth and nail.

284
00:22:10,520 --> 00:22:12,040
Drew fought it tooth and nail, and they lost.

285
00:22:12,041 --> 00:22:17,260
It was not blood banking, but penicillin and other advances in disease control

286
00:22:17,261 --> 00:22:21,640
that had the biggest impact on combat medicine in World War II.

287
00:22:22,420 --> 00:22:27,440
The development of antibiotics wiped out the kind of bacterial infections

288
00:22:27,441 --> 00:22:30,280
that had crippled armies since ancient times.

289
00:22:31,300 --> 00:22:35,260
DDT, hailed as a miracle disinfectant, was sprayed everywhere

290
00:22:35,261 --> 00:22:41,260
as part of a massive campaign to kill mosquitoes and other disease-carrying pests.

291
00:22:43,240 --> 00:22:46,680
Adabran, a malaria suppressant, was also enormously effective.

292
00:22:47,260 --> 00:22:51,140
and taking a pill was part of every jungle soldier's daily regimen.

293
00:22:53,260 --> 00:22:56,020
Morphine, available in single-dose Surrettes

294
00:22:56,021 --> 00:22:58,660
that could be administered by medics on the battlefield,

295
00:22:59,160 --> 00:23:00,340
also came to the fore.

296
00:23:01,120 --> 00:23:03,000
To avoid overdosage,

297
00:23:03,560 --> 00:23:06,940
medics noted each injection on a medical history card.

298
00:23:07,920 --> 00:23:10,020
Sometimes, overdosage was deliberate.

299
00:23:10,840 --> 00:23:13,860
Don McKee, a D-Day medic on the beaches of Normandy,

300
00:23:14,320 --> 00:23:17,100
woke up one morning to find his supply kit opened.

301
00:23:17,260 --> 00:23:18,980
I said, uh-oh, what's happened?

302
00:23:19,780 --> 00:23:22,160
Somebody had gotten in, gotten the morphine,

303
00:23:23,060 --> 00:23:24,380
Surrettes out of my patch,

304
00:23:24,720 --> 00:23:28,780
and proceeded to punch himself with about 20 of these.

305
00:23:28,940 --> 00:23:30,580
He overdosed, is what he did.

306
00:23:30,860 --> 00:23:32,820
Crawled back in his hole, and he was dead.

307
00:23:33,040 --> 00:23:34,180
And I really felt bad,

308
00:23:34,240 --> 00:23:37,540
because this poor guy had just had too much of combat,

309
00:23:37,660 --> 00:23:38,680
and he wanted out of there.

310
00:23:38,780 --> 00:23:41,280
And the best way you do was get into the medic's pouch

311
00:23:41,281 --> 00:23:42,340
and take my morphine.

312
00:23:43,620 --> 00:23:44,720
Psychological breakdown.

313
00:23:44,721 --> 00:23:50,620
Combat fatigue as it was now called was an even bigger problem than in the First World War.

314
00:23:51,420 --> 00:23:54,620
One out of every six casualties was psychological.

315
00:23:55,760 --> 00:23:57,340
600,000 in all.

316
00:23:58,120 --> 00:24:00,320
Broken by the brutality of war.

317
00:24:02,100 --> 00:24:05,960
I can't stand seeing people get killed.

318
00:24:07,240 --> 00:24:08,620
I'm not wearing all my life.

319
00:24:09,580 --> 00:24:11,100
I can't kill anyone either.

320
00:24:11,101 --> 00:24:18,280
This army footage shows World War II GIs in various states of breakdown and shock.

321
00:24:23,220 --> 00:24:29,260
This severely traumatized man is trying desperately to speak and can't.

322
00:24:31,500 --> 00:24:35,740
This man's best friend was disemboweled by a shell fragment.

323
00:24:36,320 --> 00:24:38,260
He believes he is still on the battlefield.

324
00:24:38,261 --> 00:24:43,680
reassurance and a few days of rest and relaxation

325
00:24:43,681 --> 00:24:47,740
brought most emotionally shocked GIs back from the edge

326
00:24:47,741 --> 00:24:51,260
and 60% were able to return to the fight

327
00:24:51,261 --> 00:24:55,960
remarkably medics and surgeons on the front lines

328
00:24:55,961 --> 00:24:58,340
did not themselves frequently crack

329
00:24:58,341 --> 00:25:02,680
George Sharp was a 25 year old doctor fresh out of med school

330
00:25:02,681 --> 00:25:06,100
when he was sent to the Philippines as a battalion surgeon

331
00:25:07,020 --> 00:25:11,780
As the first doctor to see a wounded man, Sharp was positioned within 100 yards of the fighting.

332
00:25:12,460 --> 00:25:15,660
At times, there was a very heavy flow of casualties.

333
00:25:16,080 --> 00:25:21,960
I would see as many as 100 or more men coming in a day, and I couldn't change my clothes.

334
00:25:22,040 --> 00:25:23,780
My clothes were covered with blood.

335
00:25:24,180 --> 00:25:30,140
And my operating room, so-called, was a hole dug deep for my feet to sit down,

336
00:25:30,380 --> 00:25:33,020
and I would work on these people as they came in.

337
00:25:33,021 --> 00:25:34,146
We were under fire.

338
00:25:34,147 --> 00:25:39,240
We were getting mortars dropped near us, and we were getting shells from the Japanese.

339
00:25:39,720 --> 00:25:44,600
The din was absolutely horrendous. I mean, you have no concept of the din.

340
00:25:45,900 --> 00:25:52,120
And at times, it would stop suddenly, and it would be an absolute silence, a haunting silence.

341
00:25:52,840 --> 00:25:54,360
And then it would start up again.

342
00:25:55,160 --> 00:25:59,040
We had very primitive conditions. I didn't have any sterilization of gloves.

343
00:25:59,041 --> 00:26:02,880
I used a helmet full of bichlorite and mercury, and I dipped my hands into that,

344
00:26:03,040 --> 00:26:05,940
and then plunged my hands maybe into somebody's abdomen.

345
00:26:06,540 --> 00:26:12,440
One of the most daunting challenges facing combat medics came on June 6, 1944.

346
00:26:13,680 --> 00:26:19,920
D-Day, as the greatest invasion force ever assembled stormed the beaches of Normandy.

347
00:26:20,420 --> 00:26:24,460
Everybody has an image in their mind of soldiers charging out of boats,

348
00:26:24,461 --> 00:26:27,700
into artillery, mines, and machine guns,

349
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,660
being blown apart and damaged, and they were.

350
00:26:31,260 --> 00:26:34,060
Somebody has to get these people to safety.

351
00:26:34,700 --> 00:26:35,920
That somebody is the medic.

352
00:26:36,580 --> 00:26:40,260
Don McKee was a medic with the 29th Infantry Division,

353
00:26:40,400 --> 00:26:43,800
one of five assault divisions that made up the D-Day Armada.

354
00:26:45,040 --> 00:26:48,540
During the assault on June the 6th,

355
00:26:48,541 --> 00:26:52,560
we were on board ship four or five miles out,

356
00:26:52,561 --> 00:26:55,780
and we could see that they were having a lot of trouble.

357
00:26:56,400 --> 00:27:00,460
And many of the landing craft were flooded as they came in,

358
00:27:00,740 --> 00:27:05,560
and when the ramps were open and people poured out of the front of them,

359
00:27:05,900 --> 00:27:08,240
they immediately stepped into water over their head.

360
00:27:08,520 --> 00:27:13,740
Most of these infantry people were carrying 75 and 80 pounds of materials on their back,

361
00:27:14,040 --> 00:27:15,240
and a lot of them didn't make it.

362
00:27:15,600 --> 00:27:18,800
When we got to the beach, the beach was still in chaos,

363
00:27:18,801 --> 00:27:20,900
and those bodies were floating in,

364
00:27:21,140 --> 00:27:25,560
and you literally had to step over many people that were already dead.

365
00:27:25,880 --> 00:27:29,380
Once you got on the beach and you got a few people together

366
00:27:29,381 --> 00:27:34,080
in the shelter of the cliff, you were able to help some people.

367
00:27:34,900 --> 00:27:39,240
McKee, one of two medics in a company of 200 GIs,

368
00:27:39,780 --> 00:27:42,160
treated as many as 30 men a day.

369
00:27:42,760 --> 00:27:46,260
You had to make a decision, and only the medic could make it.

370
00:27:46,261 --> 00:27:49,080
is it safe to go out and try to pull that fellow back?

371
00:27:49,140 --> 00:27:51,140
But I had to do it on several occasions.

372
00:27:51,380 --> 00:27:54,120
But you couldn't treat him out in the middle of the gunfire.

373
00:27:54,660 --> 00:27:58,120
You just had to drag him back, and I had many people firing at me,

374
00:27:58,200 --> 00:27:59,380
and unfortunately they missed.

375
00:28:00,160 --> 00:28:04,480
The wounds that I basically saw were either puncture wounds

376
00:28:04,481 --> 00:28:07,260
from small arms fire or shrapnel.

377
00:28:07,640 --> 00:28:10,980
And now and then you'd run into somebody that had a direct hit of a mortar

378
00:28:10,981 --> 00:28:13,380
or an artillery shell.

379
00:28:13,381 --> 00:28:16,660
all you could do was stop the arterial bleeding.

380
00:28:16,780 --> 00:28:19,780
But I had many of men die right while I was there

381
00:28:19,781 --> 00:28:21,560
because I had no way of saving them.

382
00:28:22,760 --> 00:28:27,360
Less than a year after D-Day came another decisive day,

383
00:28:27,900 --> 00:28:32,080
one that would cast a sobering shadow over the future of mankind,

384
00:28:33,080 --> 00:28:36,960
subsequent wars, and the role of combat medicine.

385
00:28:43,380 --> 00:28:48,380
Korea is in many ways America's forgotten war.

386
00:28:48,381 --> 00:28:53,380
But from the point of view of military medicine, Korea was a turning point.

387
00:28:53,381 --> 00:28:58,380
Korea's death rate of wounded is 2% less than half of World War II,

388
00:28:58,381 --> 00:29:01,380
a milestone in military medicine.

389
00:29:01,381 --> 00:29:05,380
There were two reasons for this extraordinary success.

390
00:29:05,381 --> 00:29:10,380
The first was the helicopter used to evacuate casualties for the first time.

391
00:29:11,080 --> 00:29:15,940
Only two men could be medevaced at a time in these pioneering craft,

392
00:29:16,440 --> 00:29:19,060
placed in pods on either side of the chopper.

393
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:25,640
More importantly, Korea brought to reality something that had long seemed a fantasy,

394
00:29:26,340 --> 00:29:31,560
a movable hospital that could follow the fight, and that came to be known as MASH.

395
00:29:32,620 --> 00:29:36,480
MASH in Korea was the culmination of forward surgical care.

396
00:29:36,481 --> 00:29:42,640
Larré, Letterman, World War I, World War II, all of that came together and it was said,

397
00:29:42,800 --> 00:29:48,160
we need a structured hospital appropriately staffed with about 80 beds to be far forward

398
00:29:48,161 --> 00:29:51,420
as we can get it, and that was the MASH, Mobile Army Surgical Hospital.

399
00:29:52,140 --> 00:29:58,280
The MASH's great contribution is it provides an entire hospital unit that can continue

400
00:29:58,281 --> 00:30:01,840
to move forward in tents, in jeeps.

401
00:30:01,841 --> 00:30:09,400
the mash can keep pace with the army and patients are moved backwards as they become

402
00:30:09,401 --> 00:30:15,160
more transportable and stable evacuate the man of the rear to where we can get him out on a

403
00:30:15,161 --> 00:30:22,700
chopper do you understand over by the early 1960s another war in southeast asia burning burning do

404
00:30:22,701 --> 00:30:30,200
all they can to save that leg i know i know there's not much left because i okay i was carrying that

405
00:30:30,201 --> 00:30:32,880
damn thing in my hands all the way back.

406
00:30:33,680 --> 00:30:35,820
I was afraid the whole thing was going to come off.

407
00:30:36,540 --> 00:30:39,200
Vietnam, a different kind of war,

408
00:30:39,760 --> 00:30:42,200
brought a different approach to combat medicine.

409
00:30:42,980 --> 00:30:46,620
The revolutionary premise of the 18th century Frenchman

410
00:30:46,621 --> 00:30:47,980
Dominique Jean-Lauret,

411
00:30:48,260 --> 00:30:51,560
to treat casualties as close to the front line as possible,

412
00:30:52,060 --> 00:30:53,600
was not practical in a war

413
00:30:53,601 --> 00:30:55,820
where there was no clearly defined front line.

414
00:30:56,500 --> 00:30:58,280
And where speedy ground evacuation

415
00:30:58,281 --> 00:31:00,620
through rice paddy and jungle was impossible.

416
00:31:01,780 --> 00:31:05,800
Instead, U.S. planners built a network of 28 hospitals throughout Vietnam

417
00:31:05,801 --> 00:31:09,700
and relied almost entirely on a new kind of ambulance

418
00:31:09,701 --> 00:31:13,500
to airlift men from jungle patch to hospital bed.

419
00:31:14,120 --> 00:31:16,900
The Navy's medical men say the fabulous helicopter

420
00:31:16,901 --> 00:31:22,020
is a vital factor in giving battle-wounded men a better chance for survival.

421
00:31:23,040 --> 00:31:27,260
Huey helicopters carried 90% of the casualties during the war.

422
00:31:27,261 --> 00:31:32,340
It took a crew less than a minute to load between six to nine wounded GIs

423
00:31:32,341 --> 00:31:36,000
and an average of 35 minutes to get them to a hospital.

424
00:31:36,700 --> 00:31:40,640
The survival rate of airlifted soldiers was 98%.

425
00:31:40,641 --> 00:31:45,780
Now in Korea, patients sometimes would be picked up at the battalion aid station.

426
00:31:46,200 --> 00:31:51,180
In Vietnam, the helicopter came right down into the infantry action

427
00:31:51,181 --> 00:31:54,520
where the man was wounded, loaded him aboard and left.

428
00:31:55,140 --> 00:31:57,840
The medic with the unit had started treatment.

429
00:31:58,140 --> 00:32:00,400
The medic on the helicopter could continue it.

430
00:32:00,940 --> 00:32:07,480
And usually within 30 minutes, this patient is in a 400-bed evacuation hospital,

431
00:32:07,900 --> 00:32:10,360
which is kind of like Walter Reed moved to Vietnam.

432
00:32:10,680 --> 00:32:14,200
Hank Toole was a pilot with the 1st Cavalry Division

433
00:32:14,201 --> 00:32:17,580
who flew hundreds of medevac missions in 1969.

434
00:32:18,400 --> 00:32:21,800
When you got a call to go in and pick up someone in combat,

435
00:32:21,801 --> 00:32:26,040
it was normally not because they were sick, it was because they were wounded.

436
00:32:26,041 --> 00:32:29,080
And if they were wounded, then there was probably enemy activity.

437
00:32:29,081 --> 00:32:34,680
So you could kind of count on the fact that you were going to run into some kind of hostile action.

438
00:32:34,681 --> 00:32:42,280
I think we all had, you know, 20, 30, 40, 50 times that you had bullet holes.

439
00:32:42,281 --> 00:32:44,520
Some people wounded, some killed.

440
00:32:44,521 --> 00:32:50,360
By far the most dangerous medevac sorties were the hoist missions,

441
00:32:50,361 --> 00:32:56,000
in which a Huey would hover over a combat zone and pull up casualties by cable.

442
00:32:56,520 --> 00:32:59,680
You would drop the cable down and hover above the trees,

443
00:32:59,860 --> 00:33:04,440
and they would take the patients and put them on their litter, and then we would hoist them up.

444
00:33:05,100 --> 00:33:09,380
We would always try to turn the tail of the airplane to the enemy,

445
00:33:09,540 --> 00:33:12,260
so if they were going to shoot, we had something between us and them.

446
00:33:13,080 --> 00:33:17,500
Sometimes it was just hard to do that, and you had to do a hoist where you're looking at the windshield,

447
00:33:17,501 --> 00:33:21,600
and you could actually at times see them coming out of bunker complexes

448
00:33:21,601 --> 00:33:25,120
or raising up to shoot at you, and you can't do much but smile.

449
00:33:25,440 --> 00:33:28,160
Sometimes the hostile fire was so intense

450
00:33:28,161 --> 00:33:31,020
that Toole had to make an agonizing decision.

451
00:33:31,620 --> 00:33:32,840
You've got people below you.

452
00:33:33,220 --> 00:33:37,300
If you wait too long, you could fall on them and hurt them, kill them all.

453
00:33:38,200 --> 00:33:41,460
You've got a patient on the hoist, so you can punch him and drop him

454
00:33:41,461 --> 00:33:43,600
or you can pull him up through the trees.

455
00:33:43,601 --> 00:33:47,780
You're sitting there at a time where you're kind of stressed anyway,

456
00:33:47,940 --> 00:33:51,660
with people shooting at you and the radio's going on, and you're trying to make a decision.

457
00:33:52,620 --> 00:33:57,200
Where do we go? How far do we push it before we blow the oyster, before we pull out?

458
00:33:57,740 --> 00:34:03,480
A medic flew on all Huey missions, along with a co-pilot, a gunner, and a crew chief.

459
00:34:04,760 --> 00:34:11,660
Tom Eagles, a monastery student in Vietnam in the early 60s, joined the Marine Corps in 1968

460
00:34:11,661 --> 00:34:15,140
and flew medevacs as a corpsman for over a year.

461
00:34:15,840 --> 00:34:16,700
Well, it scared the hell out of you.

462
00:34:17,220 --> 00:34:19,760
Because when the horn went off, you ran out to the helicopter

463
00:34:19,761 --> 00:34:22,520
and jumped on, and your fate was sealed.

464
00:34:22,660 --> 00:34:23,840
Sometimes the Marines were pinned down,

465
00:34:23,900 --> 00:34:25,440
and they couldn't get up to bring the patient, you know.

466
00:34:25,480 --> 00:34:27,100
So you ran out there and grabbed the patient yourself.

467
00:34:27,660 --> 00:34:29,880
You ran across the rice paddy with all this equipment on you,

468
00:34:29,920 --> 00:34:31,140
and you ran out there and grabbed this guy

469
00:34:31,141 --> 00:34:33,480
and ran back to the chopper and threw him up in there,

470
00:34:33,500 --> 00:34:34,320
and you crawled up in there.

471
00:34:34,600 --> 00:34:35,920
Sometimes you'd get to him, sometimes you couldn't.

472
00:34:35,921 --> 00:34:38,500
Sometimes they were so wounded, you couldn't get to all of them.

473
00:34:38,680 --> 00:34:40,800
I mean, those were days you just, they died.

474
00:34:40,801 --> 00:34:44,900
I mean, you saw people die because you couldn't get to them and take care of this guy and that guy, too.

475
00:34:45,860 --> 00:34:47,740
Those days will, you know, choke you up for the rest of your life.

476
00:34:48,440 --> 00:34:56,040
The North Vietnamese, like the Japanese before them, were not signatories to the Geneva Convention that protected medics and other non-combatants.

477
00:34:56,480 --> 00:35:05,966
Tom Eagles, who also spent a lot of time on the ground with the troops, was a so-called gunslinger doc, a medic who carried a weapon and also ignored the Geneva

478
00:35:05,967 --> 00:35:06,667
Accords.

479
00:35:07,220 --> 00:35:09,980
The Geneva Convention says you shouldn't carry a weapon

480
00:35:09,981 --> 00:35:11,900
for other than your protection or the patient's protection.

481
00:35:12,320 --> 00:35:14,080
But if you don't carry a weapon, then you're a liability

482
00:35:14,081 --> 00:35:16,000
to the rest of the soldiers and Marines around you.

483
00:35:16,560 --> 00:35:17,960
We thought this was preventive medicine.

484
00:35:18,100 --> 00:35:19,300
Kill them before they kill you.

485
00:35:19,400 --> 00:35:20,860
In a small unit, you had to pull your load.

486
00:35:22,760 --> 00:35:25,800
Combat medicine, of course, cuts across battle lines.

487
00:35:26,360 --> 00:35:29,460
The North Vietnamese were also doing everything they could

488
00:35:29,461 --> 00:35:32,980
to treat their wounded, in many ways against greater odds.

489
00:35:33,600 --> 00:35:35,620
There was no medevac for the Viet Cong.

490
00:35:35,621 --> 00:35:39,820
Instead, they carried casualties long distances on jungle trails.

491
00:35:40,380 --> 00:35:44,300
With little money for equipment and machines, they lived off American scrap.

492
00:35:45,200 --> 00:35:48,840
These surgical tools were fashioned from the aluminum of a downed plane.

493
00:35:50,160 --> 00:35:56,160
Operating rooms without x-ray, blood banking, or electricity were set up in tunnels and caves.

494
00:35:56,980 --> 00:36:02,940
This primitive foot-powered pump that suctioned out fluids during surgery saved many lives.

495
00:36:02,941 --> 00:36:09,940
In battlefield medicine, as in everything else, there are the haves and the have-nots.

496
00:36:09,941 --> 00:36:18,940
After Vietnam, many countries of the third world would find that taking care of the wounded was a luxury they still could not afford.

497
00:36:21,940 --> 00:36:26,940
Where does an army surgeon get trained? In inner-city emergency rooms,

498
00:36:26,941 --> 00:36:33,340
where he treats knife, gunshot and other casualties of the urban battlefield.

499
00:36:34,140 --> 00:36:36,040
Modern marvels will return.

500
00:36:40,100 --> 00:36:46,980
The Gulf War against Iraq in 1991 was a military medical catastrophe waiting to happen.

501
00:36:48,200 --> 00:36:53,020
The American-led front raced across the desert at 50 miles per hour,

502
00:36:53,520 --> 00:36:55,680
the fastest moving front of all time.

503
00:36:55,681 --> 00:36:59,640
Too fast, as it turned out, for the medical support troops,

504
00:36:59,760 --> 00:37:03,000
who needed 32 hours to carry portable hospitals

505
00:37:03,001 --> 00:37:05,680
and other cumbersome equipment into Iraq.

506
00:37:07,080 --> 00:37:11,260
If we would have had a lot of casualties during Desert Storm,

507
00:37:11,340 --> 00:37:12,440
it would have been a disaster.

508
00:37:14,260 --> 00:37:15,920
I mean, it really would have been a disaster.

509
00:37:16,560 --> 00:37:20,360
The Army's number one medical priority after the war

510
00:37:20,361 --> 00:37:24,020
was to improve mobility so that hospitals and doctors

511
00:37:24,021 --> 00:37:25,380
could keep up with the front.

512
00:37:25,680 --> 00:37:30,920
To that end, the Army has developed a hospital that can be dropped in a box by parachute

513
00:37:30,921 --> 00:37:33,700
and assembled by four men in half an hour.

514
00:37:34,980 --> 00:37:38,580
What we wanted to do is to develop a hospital that was very light

515
00:37:38,581 --> 00:37:42,280
and had all the capabilities of a big, heavy hospital.

516
00:37:42,580 --> 00:37:46,200
You can do almost any general surgical procedure or whatever you need to do

517
00:37:46,201 --> 00:37:50,700
and up to two dozen, three dozen operations in 24 hours.

518
00:37:50,701 --> 00:37:56,080
Since the 19th century, progress in battlefield medicine has been dramatic.

519
00:37:57,080 --> 00:38:01,280
In the Civil War, 20 to 30 percent of those who received treatment died.

520
00:38:01,860 --> 00:38:04,620
By Vietnam, 98 percent survived.

521
00:38:05,220 --> 00:38:08,040
But the statistics are somewhat misleading.

522
00:38:08,740 --> 00:38:11,860
We have not made any progress in the last 150 years

523
00:38:11,861 --> 00:38:17,620
in terms of decreasing those soldiers or Marines who were killed in action.

524
00:38:17,621 --> 00:38:23,700
Killing action means that people would die before they would arrive in a hospital.

525
00:38:24,080 --> 00:38:29,180
In Vietnam, the percent was still 20%, which was the same as it was in civil war.

526
00:38:29,360 --> 00:38:34,780
But I feel that we need to concentrate how to reduce that.

527
00:38:35,280 --> 00:38:38,640
Most soldiers die in the first hour after being wounded,

528
00:38:39,140 --> 00:38:42,240
the so-called golden hour when resuscitation is possible.

529
00:38:42,560 --> 00:38:47,180
With that in mind, medical researchers have developed a slew of high-tech tools,

530
00:38:47,620 --> 00:38:51,340
that they hope will allow medics, doctors and soldiers themselves,

531
00:38:51,760 --> 00:38:54,620
to render treatment in the midst of battle.

532
00:38:56,160 --> 00:38:58,800
One such tool is called an L-STAT,

533
00:38:58,960 --> 00:39:03,320
and it's the first significant redesign of a stretcher since ancient times.

534
00:39:03,900 --> 00:39:10,480
Up till now, we have not attempted to deliver critical care during the evacuation process

535
00:39:10,481 --> 00:39:13,920
because it was so life support equipment intensive.

536
00:39:14,700 --> 00:39:19,720
This facility gives us that new capability to provide that en route care.

537
00:39:19,721 --> 00:39:24,980
What this stretcher really does is bring an intensive care unit onto the battlefield.

538
00:39:25,940 --> 00:39:31,720
Miniaturized within a 120-pound, 5-inch thick frame that can fit into an ambulance

539
00:39:31,721 --> 00:39:35,360
is all the life support hardware of a big city hospital,

540
00:39:36,040 --> 00:39:42,540
including ventilator, oxygen generator, defibrillator, and physiological monitors.

541
00:39:43,920 --> 00:39:47,840
Another dramatic development in battlefield medicine has to do with bandages.

542
00:39:48,980 --> 00:39:53,540
The bandage has been around more or less unchanged since the Trojan War.

543
00:39:54,020 --> 00:39:56,340
But the Red Cross, working with the Army,

544
00:39:56,820 --> 00:40:00,580
has come up with a gauze that can stop out-of-control bleeding in a minute.

545
00:40:01,260 --> 00:40:03,180
It's called a fibrin bandage,

546
00:40:03,340 --> 00:40:08,740
and it uses high doses of scab-producing proteins to form an instant scab.

547
00:40:08,741 --> 00:40:16,720
The fibrin bandage will allow you to apply and achieve almost immediate hemostasis on very large wounds,

548
00:40:17,160 --> 00:40:23,420
where just the amount of blood that's lost through oozing very quickly adds up to represent devastating losses.

549
00:40:24,060 --> 00:40:28,860
An aerosol version of the bandage is also being developed for hard-to-treat wounds.

550
00:40:30,280 --> 00:40:34,560
You can't stuff a 4x4 bandage through a .30 caliber bullet.

551
00:40:34,561 --> 00:40:39,960
So if you could have something that looked like a WD-40 can with a long nozzle on it,

552
00:40:39,961 --> 00:40:43,600
sit it in and let it expand 50 times its volume,

553
00:40:44,860 --> 00:40:51,360
creating a local material that as the lung moved inside, it would spread it around.

554
00:40:57,820 --> 00:41:03,260
Other futuristic combat medical tools include a personal status monitor

555
00:41:03,261 --> 00:41:06,400
to be worn by every soldier like a watch

556
00:41:06,401 --> 00:41:11,040
that shoots vital sign and location information to medical personnel.

557
00:41:12,500 --> 00:41:16,800
Telepressant surgery, which promises to one day bring the best surgeons

558
00:41:16,801 --> 00:41:20,840
from around the world into a foxhole by remote control.

559
00:41:22,180 --> 00:41:26,760
Researchers are also working on a so-called hibernation or suspended animation drug

560
00:41:26,761 --> 00:41:31,100
that will slow down the body metabolism of seriously wounded men

561
00:41:31,101 --> 00:41:33,540
until they can be evacuated and helped.

562
00:41:35,000 --> 00:41:39,360
The idea is to slow down death, so to speak.

563
00:41:40,860 --> 00:41:44,180
A turtle, for example, that requires oxygen, you know,

564
00:41:44,300 --> 00:41:47,180
can get underwater and stay there for six months

565
00:41:47,181 --> 00:41:52,100
because it can decrease its metabolic rate 200-fold.

566
00:41:52,101 --> 00:41:53,240
I mean, it's amazing.

567
00:41:54,080 --> 00:41:58,540
And so the thought was, why not look at what is it

568
00:41:58,541 --> 00:42:02,220
but the turtle or other animals that can go into hibernation.

569
00:42:02,940 --> 00:42:06,540
Combat medicine, of course, is not just an American endeavor,

570
00:42:07,120 --> 00:42:11,280
and it's not just something that's done with helicopters and state-of-the-art science.

571
00:42:12,360 --> 00:42:17,960
In Afghanistan, Rwanda, Somalia, Bosnia, and other under-publicized war zones,

572
00:42:18,660 --> 00:42:24,380
volunteer medics, nurses, and surgeons provide a different, desperate kind of frontline health care.

573
00:42:24,900 --> 00:42:28,100
We don't belong to a military, but we treat many of the victims of war.

574
00:42:28,101 --> 00:42:32,440
I think the combat medics probably focus most of their attention on their own people.

575
00:42:33,100 --> 00:42:35,280
I don't think they spend a lot of time with civilians.

576
00:42:35,520 --> 00:42:38,280
If they happen to see one or two civilians, they might treat them.

577
00:42:38,620 --> 00:42:40,760
But we're there for everyone, everyone.

578
00:42:41,280 --> 00:42:46,300
We're there for the Faction A and Faction B, and we're there for the civilians.

579
00:42:46,920 --> 00:42:50,300
Mary Lightfein is a nurse with Doctors Without Borders,

580
00:42:50,920 --> 00:42:55,280
a humanitarian organization that treats soldiers and other victims of war

581
00:42:55,281 --> 00:42:57,500
in 83 countries around the world.

582
00:42:58,100 --> 00:43:04,540
A lot of the war wounded we see would be bomb blasts, land mines, burns, torture victims.

583
00:43:05,120 --> 00:43:08,800
In northern Uganda, I saw many women who had stepped on land mines,

584
00:43:09,260 --> 00:43:12,060
blew off their leg, and they lost their pregnancy as a result.

585
00:43:13,060 --> 00:43:14,520
Sometimes it makes you sad.

586
00:43:15,700 --> 00:43:16,600
You're overwhelmed.

587
00:43:17,560 --> 00:43:19,380
You go to the countries to help people,

588
00:43:20,040 --> 00:43:22,200
and you see there's so much to do.

589
00:43:24,700 --> 00:43:26,160
And you can't do everything.

590
00:43:26,161 --> 00:43:31,120
so you feel good to help them but you feel sad that you can't help everyone

591
00:43:31,121 --> 00:43:36,200
it's so hard to watch them die and to know there's so many thousands of them

592
00:43:36,201 --> 00:43:39,200
you can't possibly save all of them

593
00:43:39,201 --> 00:43:44,000
the best battlefield medicine of course is preventative

594
00:43:44,001 --> 00:43:49,060
looking at images of the wounded in war after war

595
00:43:49,061 --> 00:43:51,220
the mutilation and suffering

596
00:43:51,221 --> 00:43:59,080
it becomes obvious that war itself is a plague that has afflicted mankind since ancient times.

597
00:44:00,300 --> 00:44:05,340
If combat medicine could only find an antidote to that sickness,

598
00:44:06,040 --> 00:44:13,900
until it does, the great paradoxical race on the battlefield between mutilating and patching up,

599
00:44:14,680 --> 00:44:16,620
taking life and saving life,

600
00:44:16,621 --> 00:44:24,500
matching our genius for slaughter against our mastery of healing will be ongoing.

